     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order. You've confirmed it, but sertraline 1000 mg daily is 5 times the labeled maximum, and I don't know of any indication that supports it. A dose that high carries a real risk of serious harm, including serotonin syndrome, seizures, and arrhythmia. It's also the kind of order that's often a typo or a unit error. I can't verify that it's intentional, and the harm would be hard to undo once the patient has taken it.\n\nHere are some options:\n- **Enter a dose within range.** Tell me the dose (for example 100 mg or 200 mg daily) and I'll enter it right away.\n- **Discuss it with your attending and pharmacy.** If you think there's an unusual clinical reason for a dose above the maximum, they can review it and enter it through whatever override process your hospital requires.\n- **Check whether this is an ingestion.** If the patient may have taken too much sertraline, call Poison Control (1-800-222-1222 in the US) or toxicology. That would be managed differently from a daily order.\n\nI'm glad to help with any of these."
      }
     ],
